From 70c41f94dbd7db42326b9fc672ff743adeba1f88 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: desocketed <desocketed@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 17:05:41 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 24/25] vk_sync: the submit finish wait and the LOD guard wait
 for the GPU, not for the completion callbacks (NX2NAVI_GPU_WAIT)

The completion thread published `done` only after it had run every callback up to the value the timeline reached:
the write-back compares (5.4 ms per field frame on Cal's 2026-11-09 Tracy trace), descriptor pool resets, frees,
vk_mem_collect. The game thread's `submit finish wait` after a frame's first submission and the LOD guard's wait
(both on fence_wait, so on `done`) therefore paid for that CPU work on top of the GPU time.

A second counter, `reached_gpu`, is stored (release, under st_lock, cv_done broadcast) right after
vkGetSemaphoreCounterValue and before the callback loop. vk_sync_gpu_wait / vk_sync_gpu_done wait on it, through
the new GfxBackend gpu_wait / gpu_done (the null back end aliases its fence functions). NX2NAVI_GPU_WAIT=0 makes
both read `done`, the old behaviour.

Which waiters move: only the two whose purpose is to order the GPU's *reads* of guest memory before the game rewrites
it. The submit finish wait exists because Ocarina rewrites the data its frame-start passes read as soon as the submit
returns (white dialogue frames); the LOD guard because the producer rewrites the back half of its per-object uniform
blocks once the previous flip has been consumed. On hardware the guarantee they stand in for is "the GPU is through
with these commands", which `reached_gpu` is exactly. Neither reads anything the GPU wrote.

Which waiters stay on `done`: everything that consumes GPU results on the CPU. nvnSyncWait and nvnQueueFinish
(fence_wait, fence_done; the game's own synchronisation before it reads counters, query results or GPU-written
buffers), the texture read-back and guest-read paths in vk_backend.c (vk_texture_dirty_fence, the mirror and shadow
write-back into guest memory), vk_usnap, the pipeline-set waits in vk_exec_draw.c, the event waits. The shadow
write-back (vk_gpu_written, run from the submission's completion callback) still lands before `done`, so a CPU read of
GPU-written CPU-visible memory that went through a sync sees it as before.

What changes in timing: the first submission's write-back now lands a few ms after the game thread resumes instead of
before. A CPU write into a GPU-written page in that window would be overwritten by the write-back (the compare sees a
differing page and copies the shadow's bytes). That window already exists for every other submission of the frame
(none of them is waited for), and the game writes such pages (the sky-SH block it zeroes) at the start of the next
frame, ~20 ms later in the field, so it does not come near it; NX2NAVI_GPU_WAIT=0 is the way back if a scene does.
